WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.180
Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.630
My name is Sam, and
I'm a graduate student

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.730
in Comparative Media Studies.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:10.260
I have a background in animation
and public health research,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.730
and I'm fascinated by people who
can take big, complex history

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.140
topics and offer them up for
discussion in artistic formats.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.320
So I'm really excited to
be speaking with Rosa today

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.390
for a CAST conversation.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:30.160
And I'd love to give Rosa a
chance for a self-introduction.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.680
Hi.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.830
I'm Rosa, and I am an
illustrator comic artist

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:36.880
from Puerto Rico.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.410
I live in San Juan.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:41.530
And I have been self-publishing
comics for 10 years.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:47.200
And recently I've switched
over to political comics, where

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.150
I try to write a comic
about our experiences

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.630
in the island, our politics,
and all the problems that

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:00.430
have surfaced after
Hurricane Maria specifically.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.430
And I usually publish
mostly on The Nib,

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:08.200
but I've also been published
on the Believer and The Lily

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.228
for The Washington Post.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.900
So my first question
that sort of

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.970
brings us here today
is pretty basic,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.670
but what brought you
to MIT, and how is it

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.880
influencing your work?

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.900
Well, I was invited by
CAST and Michael Jacobson

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:34.320
to work for the MIT Game Lab on
illustrations for a board game

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.460
based on PROMESA, the
PROMESA law in Puerto Rico.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:45.420
So the PROMESA law is a law that
President Obama signed way back

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:51.480
in 2016, and it imposes a
fiscal oversight and control

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.910
board over our government.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:57.990
And it's been a huge-- of course
it's been a huge issue here.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.780
And I wrote a little bit
about it on my comics.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.200
And so Michael
contacted me based

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.120
on that because
they wanted somebody

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.009
who was native to the
problem and to the country

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:14.790
to do the illustrations
for this board game.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:20.550
Oh, and I'm also interested in
sort of this shift from some

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:26.210
of the more 2D, static
artistic formats

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:30.240
that you said that
you're rooted in

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.750
and how the interactivity
and the collaboration

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.973
of this project affects your
approach to covering the topic.

00:02:38.973 --> 00:02:40.390
Well, it's been
really interesting

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.930
because I've never worked
on a game board before,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.350
and I hardly even
play them, even.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:54.840
So it was very interesting to
figure out how to tell a story.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.340
I cannot tell right
now if it's just my--

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.110
OK, it looks like
Rosa is frozen.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.550
It's not just me.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:08.820
So while I think we wait
for Rosa to join us,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.640
I think I can use
this time to set up

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.670
the next line of
questioning that I have.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:18.480
Basically I had the
wonderful opportunity

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.260
to play test the
PROMESA board game

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.660
that the Game Lab is
working on and that Rosa

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.570
is contributing to.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.890
And the design of it, to
me, is really fascinating.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:39.450
It takes this complex story
of the law that Rosa was just

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:44.940
describing and puts you in
the position, maybe generally

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.360
the Puerto Rican
government, trying

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.520
to make balances
between spending money

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:56.850
on infrastructure and
education and paying off

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.940
its debt to the US.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.100
And the debt is sort of in the
form of these physical jewels

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.520
that you're trying to
push off of the map.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.140
But in order to
do that, you also

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.170
need to spend some
of those jewels

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.290
on education, on infrastructure.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.040
Rosa is back.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:17.624
Cool.

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:19.920
Yes, I'm sorry.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.490
The internet, it's crazy here.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.370
Yeah.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.340
I fixed it, so hopefully
it won't happen again.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:27.840
All right.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.470
I was talking a little
bit about my experience

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.710
play testing the game and just
the way that it kind of works.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.720
I think we can
circle back to some

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.900
of the experience
with the medium

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.880
as a part of this question.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.850
But I was essentially
sort of talking

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.610
about how one thing that stuck
out to me about this game

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:55.980
was how really closely tied
the topic and the format

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.200
are compared to other games that
I've had the chance to test out

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.520
with the Game lab and their
anti-colonial board game

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.220
project, sort of playing
other board games that

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.955
approach this topic.

00:05:09.955 --> 00:05:12.330
One thing that stuck out to
me about a lot of those games

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.690
is that the colonialist
themes usually have nothing

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.610
to do with the actual
gameplay or the interactions,

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.870
and rather than make some sort
of educational or aesthetic

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.420
or emotional statement
about colonialism,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.310
those games usually
just use the theme

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:30.510
as an excuse to have fun
while collecting treasure.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.500
And so it was really refreshing
for me to see this board game

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.890
dive into the tricky questions
of how to educate and offer

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.720
a meaningful
aesthetic experience

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.600
and highlight the emotional
experience of balancing

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.190
infrastructure and education and
debt at that government level.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.820
And so my next question
is related to that.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.850
In contributing your
artwork to this game,

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.460
I really want to know
what it's like to try

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.380
to do all of those
things at once

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.560
and have the artwork
also help people

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.070
learn how to play the game.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.153
And so I know you got cut
off a little bit in sort

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:08.445
of explaining how the
format affects you,

00:06:08.445 --> 00:06:10.320
but I think this can
sort of all be rolled up

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.555
into one big question.

00:06:12.555 --> 00:06:13.590
Yeah, I agree.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:14.850
I agree.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.260
Yeah, I mean, Michael
didn't explain to me

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:23.790
how the game was before I got
to MIT for the quick visit,

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.440
and I think he was
right because you

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.280
have to actually experience it.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.450
Because it's-- I was also kind
of expecting it to be like,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.423
well, I'm going to
educate you about this,

00:06:35.423 --> 00:06:36.840
and you're going
to roll the dice,

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.180
and you're going to get
from point A to point B,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:39.990
and that's it.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:46.410
But I think what they created
is very creative because it puts

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:50.310
the person in the position
of having to actually analyze

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:55.470
on how to spend the
resources of a whole country,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.670
but in a very simple way.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:02.880
So one of my challenges is how
to contextualize that so it's

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.140
even more obvious so that when
the game asks you to invest

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:13.200
in infrastructure, then,
well, what specific example

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.000
of Puerto Rico's
history can I use

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.100
there so that the person
can engage with history

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.260
as well as engage with the game?

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.140
And because I do comics,
I'm very used to, well,

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:23.700
I'll walk you through the story.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:27.150
But now the people actually
have to play the game,

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:31.500
and it's very cooperative,
too, like you noticed.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.100
So what I think
my challenge is is

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.220
how to present Puerto Rico
in a non-stereotypical way,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:42.420
how to contextualize the history
so that the person understands

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.430
how to play it, but at the same
time learn something about how,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.370
well, if we put the
resources from education

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:51.960
to infrastructure,
then you lose this,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.040
and this is going to happen.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.248
It could end up in a protest.

00:07:54.248 --> 00:07:57.140
It could end up in
a teacher strike.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:59.390
But also, one of
the things that I'm

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.580
very excited to work
on with this project

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.650
is that Michael also wanted a
comic book as the instructions.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:09.200
But then the comic
book is not only

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.870
just going to be the
instructions on how to play.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:16.400
It'll be a conversation between
two players about the context

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.980
of the history of the game.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.860
So I'm very excited to
be able to put something

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:25.040
that I'm very comfortable
with into the game

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.440
at the same time while
being challenged with all

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.370
of the illustrations and kind
of the decisions of what I want

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.830
to illustrate there so that
the person takes something

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:38.659
away from the game more than
the enjoyment of playing.

00:08:38.659 --> 00:08:42.409
Oh, and I think that leads
me into my final question

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:46.100
of there's a lot to
unpack in this game.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.750
I think it'll mean
a lot depending on

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.690
who plays it, in what
context, and when, even.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:54.500
And so my last question
to you is, who do you

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.390
want to play this game?

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.340
I want everybody
to play the game,

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.850
but I know that everybody
here is super excited.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.890
The moment I said
that I was working

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.430
on a board game
about Puerto Rico,

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:08.055
people were like,
but what is it about?

00:09:08.055 --> 00:09:09.350
What-- I need to know.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.350
Do you have a prototype?

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.570
And they were so excited.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:18.210
So I would really love
to have, once everything

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:21.120
is done to a point,
I would really

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.550
love to have a community--

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.390
after the pandemic,
after everything--

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:31.800
a community play so that people
here can add their own stories

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.910
to the game and maybe start--

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:40.410
I think this game
can probably have

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:44.790
a type of closure or healing
on some of these topics.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:46.920
Oh, yeah, like
they could go, oh,

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.930
I remember when we
protested about this,

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.930
or I remember how I felt when
the government announced this

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.900
and that, but they are
also working together

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:59.070
and realize that, oh, yeah,
if we do invest in education,

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.270
we might have a way out.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.990
So it sounds like a bit
of a grim game saying,

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:07.050
oh, this is just to push
the debt off the island,

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.210
and the debt is so huge
that it's unpayable,

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.502
and you're going to learn that.

00:10:10.502 --> 00:10:14.570
But I think because
it's cooperative,

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:19.740
it has a way to maybe help form
a conversation so that people

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.090
can get a little bit of
closure and a little bit

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:28.340
of recontextualization on their
feelings and of their history.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.020
I'm very excited to play here.

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:34.650
Yeah, I'm excited to play
the final game as well.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:35.150
Great.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:35.810
Thanks so much.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:38.490
This was a really
fantastic conversation.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.470
It was really inspiring to learn
more about your own approach

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.340
to this work, how the
dialogue around the project

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.570
and your exposure to
some of the issues

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.730
that you're talking about,
getting that into MIT

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.700
and making that part of the
conversation around here.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.190
It's something that I
really appreciated seeing.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:00.305
So thank you so much,
and thanks for the--

00:11:00.305 --> 00:11:03.140
No, thank everybody for letting
me work on this project.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.490
Thank you, Sam and Rosa.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.440
And thanks, everyone,
for joining us

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.220
for this conversation.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:17.520
Please visit
arts.mit.edu/fromanywhere

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.560
for a wide range
of arts activities,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.350
online exhibitions and
performances, research,

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:23.790
and more.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:26.460
Also, keep an eye on our
website for more information

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.560
about this board game and the
developments in the future.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:32.190
Thanks again, everyone, and
have a wonderful weekend.

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:33.180
Goodbye.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:34.730
Bye.

